1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,723
A steel spacecraft taller than a fifteen-story
building is falling back to Earth.

2
00:00:05,763 --> 00:00:10,207
And waiting for it, there's no
runway. No parachute. No ocean.

3
00:00:10,247 --> 00:00:13,017
Just two giant arms, on a tower.

4
00:00:13,317 --> 00:00:15,078
This hasn't happened yet.

5
00:00:15,118 --> 00:00:19,774
SpaceX has already caught a falling
rocket booster with a tower like this.

6
00:00:19,814 --> 00:00:21,149
Three times.

7
00:00:21,189 --> 00:00:25,179
But on its next flight, it says it
will try to catch the spacecraft too.

8
00:00:25,219 --> 00:00:27,360
The part that comes back from orbit.

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,090
And that second catch is a
completely different problem.

10
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:34,418
Here's the whole machine.
Starship is really two rockets,

11
00:00:34,458 --> 00:00:36,329
stacked on top of each other.

12
00:00:36,369 --> 00:00:40,911
At the bottom is Super Heavy, the booster.
Thirty-three engines, and one job:

13
00:00:40,951 --> 00:00:42,947
get everything off the ground.

14
00:00:42,987 --> 00:00:46,953
On top is the ship. The part
that actually goes to space.

15
00:00:46,993 --> 00:00:51,391
Stacked together, they stand about a
hundred and twenty-four meters tall.

16
00:00:51,431 --> 00:00:55,005
For most of history, rockets like
this were simply thrown away.

17
00:00:55,045 --> 00:00:58,856
Even Falcon Nine only
brings back its first stage.

18
00:00:58,896 --> 00:01:02,428
On October twentieth, Elon
Musk said that Flight Fifteen,

19
00:01:02,468 --> 00:01:06,299
expected as soon as November,
will attempt to catch both.

20
00:01:06,339 --> 00:01:10,932
If it works, the entire rocket comes
home to the place it launched from.

21
00:01:11,233 --> 00:01:16,812
To see why the ship is so much harder,
start with the part SpaceX has already done.

22
00:01:16,852 --> 00:01:20,402
A few minutes after liftoff,
the booster's work is finished.

23
00:01:20,442 --> 00:01:24,798
The ship lights its own engines while
it's still attached, and pushes away.

24
00:01:24,838 --> 00:01:26,769
That's called hot staging.

25
00:01:26,809 --> 00:01:31,013
Now the booster is heading the wrong
way: up, and out over the ocean.

26
00:01:31,053 --> 00:01:35,612
So it flips around, relights some of its
engines, and burns back toward the coast.

27
00:01:35,652 --> 00:01:37,228
That's the boostback.

28
00:01:37,268 --> 00:01:39,859
Then the engines go quiet, and it falls.

29
00:01:39,899 --> 00:01:43,235
On the way down, it steers
with grid fins near its top.

30
00:01:43,275 --> 00:01:45,071
They look like giant waffle irons,

31
00:01:45,111 --> 00:01:49,412
and they bite into the air to point
the booster where it needs to go.

32
00:01:49,452 --> 00:01:54,521
On final approach, it lights its
engines one last time to slow down.

33
00:01:54,561 --> 00:01:57,416
And here's the part most
people picture wrong.

34
00:01:57,456 --> 00:02:00,096
The tower doesn't reach
out and grab the rocket.

35
00:02:00,136 --> 00:02:02,878
The rocket flies itself into the tower.

36
00:02:02,918 --> 00:02:04,839
The arms, nicknamed the chopsticks,

37
00:02:04,879 --> 00:02:08,704
swing in underneath a load-bearing
point near the top of the booster,

38
00:02:08,744 --> 00:02:11,190
and the booster settles down onto them.

39
00:02:11,230 --> 00:02:13,725
They're not squeezing
a slippery steel tube.

40
00:02:13,765 --> 00:02:16,633
They're giving a rocket
a ledge to stand on.

41
00:02:16,673 --> 00:02:20,627
SpaceX first pulled this off in
October of twenty twenty-four,

42
00:02:20,667 --> 00:02:23,311
and twice more in twenty twenty-five.

43
00:02:23,351 --> 00:02:28,165
The newest version changes the hardware:
three bigger grid fins instead of four,

44
00:02:28,205 --> 00:02:33,079
and a new catch point. The tower's arms
got shorter too, with electric actuators,

45
00:02:33,119 --> 00:02:36,388
so they can move faster
to track the rocket.

46
00:02:36,588 --> 00:02:38,472
But the booster's whole trip is short.

47
00:02:38,512 --> 00:02:42,122
It never goes anywhere near
fast enough to stay in orbit.

48
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:43,439
The ship does.

49
00:02:43,479 --> 00:02:45,342
And orbit isn't about going high.

50
00:02:45,382 --> 00:02:48,604
It's about going sideways
so fast that as you fall,

51
00:02:48,644 --> 00:02:50,994
the Earth curves away beneath you.

52
00:02:51,034 --> 00:02:55,398
For a low orbit, that's around
twenty-eight thousand kilometers an hour.

53
00:02:55,438 --> 00:03:00,587
To come home, all of that speed has to go
somewhere. Most of it goes into the air.

54
00:03:00,627 --> 00:03:02,881
The ship falls back with its belly first.

55
00:03:02,921 --> 00:03:06,907
The air in front of it is
squeezed so hard that it glows.

56
00:03:06,947 --> 00:03:12,686
That's why one side of the ship is covered in
thousands of black hexagonal heat shield tiles.

57
00:03:12,726 --> 00:03:14,853
That side faces the fire.

58
00:03:14,893 --> 00:03:19,878
Four flaps, two near the nose and two at
the tail, steer it through the descent,

59
00:03:19,918 --> 00:03:23,193
a bit like a skydiver
using their arms and legs.

60
00:03:23,233 --> 00:03:27,241
Then, close to the ground, comes
the strangest move in rocketry.

61
00:03:27,281 --> 00:03:31,457
The ship lights its engines and
swings from horizontal to vertical.

62
00:03:31,497 --> 00:03:35,459
A fifty-meter spacecraft,
flipping upright in a few seconds.

63
00:03:35,499 --> 00:03:39,544
SpaceX has flown that flip before:
on low test hops years ago,

64
00:03:39,584 --> 00:03:45,116
and after trips to space that ended in
the ocean. It has never ended at a tower.

65
00:03:45,156 --> 00:03:50,134
This time, the ship has to survive the
heat of reentry first, and then still flip,

66
00:03:50,174 --> 00:03:52,707
slow down and line up next to a tower,

67
00:03:52,747 --> 00:03:56,033
with the same kind of
precision the booster needed.

68
00:03:56,333 --> 00:03:58,247
So let's go inside the tower.

69
00:03:58,287 --> 00:04:02,780
It's a steel lattice, roughly as
tall as a forty-five-story building.

70
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:06,404
The arms hang from a carriage
that rides up and down the tower,

71
00:04:06,444 --> 00:04:08,774
so the catch height can be adjusted.

72
00:04:08,814 --> 00:04:12,615
And each arm swings open
and closed on its own hinge.

73
00:04:12,655 --> 00:04:15,145
Before the ship arrives,
the arms open wide,

74
00:04:15,185 --> 00:04:17,918
and the carriage waits
at the right height.

75
00:04:17,958 --> 00:04:22,818
The ship drops in beside the tower,
between the arms. In the last few seconds,

76
00:04:22,858 --> 00:04:25,819
the arms close in
beneath its catch points.

77
00:04:25,859 --> 00:04:30,186
The engines shut down, and the ship's
weight comes down onto the arms.

78
00:04:30,226 --> 00:04:35,541
SpaceX hasn't published a detailed drawing
of how the ship's catch points meet the arms,

79
00:04:35,581 --> 00:04:39,174
so what you're seeing here
is our conceptual version.

80
00:04:39,214 --> 00:04:42,994
But it has rehearsed part of
this on the ground. In September,

81
00:04:43,034 --> 00:04:44,843
according to NASA Spaceflight,

82
00:04:44,883 --> 00:04:49,834
the arms at Starbase's second pad lifted
Ship forty-two, the Flight Fifteen ship,

83
00:04:49,874 --> 00:04:52,106
into a simulated catch position.

84
00:04:52,146 --> 00:04:56,315
That matters, because the ship isn't
shaped like the booster. It's shorter,

85
00:04:56,355 --> 00:05:00,488
it has flaps sticking out, and its
heat shield wraps around one side.

86
00:05:00,528 --> 00:05:04,248
The arms have to close around all
of that without hitting any of it.

87
00:05:04,288 --> 00:05:08,374
So everything has to arrive together.
The right place, the right height,

88
00:05:08,414 --> 00:05:11,182
the right speed, pointing the right way.

89
00:05:11,482 --> 00:05:15,115
So what could go wrong? Here,
we're reasoning from geometry,

90
00:05:15,155 --> 00:05:19,432
because SpaceX hasn't published
its rules for calling off a catch.

91
00:05:19,472 --> 00:05:24,416
If the ship arrives a little too far out,
its catch points won't be over the arms.

92
00:05:24,456 --> 00:05:27,869
If it's leaning, one side
touches down before the other.

93
00:05:27,909 --> 00:05:32,609
If it's still coming down too fast,
the tower has to soak up more energy.

94
00:05:32,649 --> 00:05:36,408
And if the tower isn't ready,
there's nothing to land on at all.

95
00:05:36,448 --> 00:05:41,271
That last one has actually happened. On
Flight Six, a booster catch was called off,

96
00:05:41,311 --> 00:05:44,266
and the booster came
down in the Gulf instead.

97
00:05:44,306 --> 00:05:47,119
It would make sense for the
ship to have a similar way out.

98
00:05:47,159 --> 00:05:49,186
But the details aren't public.

99
00:05:49,226 --> 00:05:50,826
And on earlier test flights,

100
00:05:50,866 --> 00:05:54,094
ships have been lost on the
way up and on the way down.

101
00:05:54,134 --> 00:05:58,028
The ship has always been the
harder half of this program.

102
00:05:58,328 --> 00:06:02,531
So if everything goes right, Flight
Fifteen looks something like this.

103
00:06:02,571 --> 00:06:07,161
Liftoff. The booster separates, turns
back, and minutes later, it's caught.

104
00:06:07,201 --> 00:06:10,102
The ship keeps going,
all the way to space.

105
00:06:10,142 --> 00:06:13,173
Later, it falls back through
the heat, flips upright,

106
00:06:13,213 --> 00:06:15,384
and drops between a pair of arms.

107
00:06:15,424 --> 00:06:18,579
Musk said he thinks success
is more likely than not.

108
00:06:18,619 --> 00:06:22,408
He also said there are more
flights lined up if it fails.

109
00:06:22,448 --> 00:06:26,468
And even a perfect catch is not the
finish line. To make this routine,

110
00:06:26,508 --> 00:06:31,639
a caught ship has to be inspected, refueled,
and flown again, without being rebuilt.

111
00:06:31,679 --> 00:06:33,029
But if both catches work,

112
00:06:33,069 --> 00:06:38,832
Musk says it would be the first time an
orbital rocket has ever been recovered in full.

113
00:06:38,872 --> 00:06:43,148
For as long as there have been rockets,
they've been built to be thrown away.

114
00:06:43,188 --> 00:06:45,335
This one is built to be caught.
